{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-97023",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maxima of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0\\le x\\le 8$ is $f(x)=\\frac{x(6-x)^{2}}{128}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(6-x)^{2}-2x(6-x)}{128} \\\\  & =\\frac{(6-x)(6-x-2x)}{128} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(6-x)(2-x)}{128},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{3(6-x)(2-x)}{128}\\,⟹\\,x=2,6.\n$$\n\nAll of these critical points are within the interval $[0,8],$ so we keep them all.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=0,8.$ So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,2,6,8:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{0(6-0)^{2}}{128}=0 \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{2(6-2)^{2}}{128}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ f(6) & =\\frac{6(6-6)^{2}}{128}=0 \\\\ f(8) & =\\frac{8(6-8)^{2}}{128}=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest values are\n\n$$\nf(2)=f(8)=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $2$ and $8.$",
